{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3182","artifact_type":"architect","name":"plan/architect-backlog-add-silent-failure.md@sha256:1483721b220126245e6c64ef1917be70fa806298901c1baa7b0c42634f26bc69"},"owner_reference":"3182","content":"# Architecture: backlog_add silent failure on issue creation (#3182)\n\n## Decision\n\nPopulate the existing, already-wired-but-unused `BacklogAddResponse.error` field (and\n`add_item`'s result dict `\"error\"` key) **without raising** and **without removing the\nlocal-only-item fallback**. This is the \"populate the existing `error` field\" option\nnamed in the delegation brief, not the \"raise\" option \u2014 see Rejected Alternative below\nfor why the beads raise-based precedent does not transfer to this call site.\n\nZero new Pydantic fields, zero new exception classes, zero schema changes. The fix is\nentirely: (1) two `operations.py` functions start returning the failure reason they\nalready compute instead of discarding it after `out.warn()`, and (2) `add_item` copies\nthat reason into the result dict under the key the response model already reserves for\nit.\n\n## Rejected Alternative: raise (match the beads precedent)\n\nThe Root-Cause Analysis section correctly identifies that `BeadsBackend.put_work_item`\nraises `ContentUnavailableError` when native issue creation fails, and asks whether the\ninteger-ID path (GitHub/sqlite/memory) should match that shape. It should not, for two\nindependent reasons verified against the current tree:\n\n1. **A raise here deletes a supported, tested feature.**\n   `test_add_item_local_only_pending_state_visible_via_list_and_view`\n   (`tests/test_backlog_core_operations.py:435-459`) mocks `try_get_github` to return\n   `None` and asserts `add_item` **succeeds**, producing a local-only item whose pending\n   state is later visible via `list_items`/`view_item`. This is issue #2999's shipped\n   behavior, not the defect. Beads has no such feature \u2014 a beads item *is* the backend\n   record, so there is no \"local-only\" fallback to preserve, and raising is beads'\n   correct (only) option. GitHub/sqlite/memory backends do have that fallback, and it\n   is load-bearing. Raising on GitHub-unavailable would regress this test.\n\n2. **`ContentUnavailableError` is not a `BacklogError` and is not caught at either call\n   site.** `ContentUnavailableError(ContentProviderError(Exception))` is a disjoint\n   hierarchy from `BacklogError(Exception)` (`backlog_core/models.py:486,494,498`). Both\n   production call sites \u2014 `backlog_add` (`server.py:1404`, `except BacklogError as e`)\n   and CLI `add` (`sam_schema/backlog.py:74`, `except BacklogError as exc`) \u2014 only catch\n   `BacklogError`. Reusing `ContentUnavailableError` here would propagate as an unhandled\n   exception through both call sites instead of producing the structured response the\n   acceptance criteria require. (This appears to be a latent, pre-existing gap on the\n   beads path too \u2014 `BeadsBackend.put_work_item`'s `ContentUnavailableError` would hit\n   the same uncaught path if `add_item` reached it through `backlog_add`/CLI `add`. Out\n   of scope here; worth its own backlog item \u2014 see Concerns.)\n\nA raise-based fix could theoretically work if paired with an exception subclassed under\n`BacklogError` \u2014 but that still breaks reason (1). Populating `error` without raising is\nthe only option consistent with both the existing local-only-create contract and the\nexisting exception-catching wiring.\n\n## A further narrowing: \"unavailable\" vs. \"creation failed\" are not the same failure\n\nThe Acceptance Criteria section asks that *both* \"GitHub issue creation fails\" *and*\n\"GitHub is unavailable\" populate a caller-visible error. Implementing that literally\nbreaks a second, wider surface \u2014 see Concerns for why this spec deliberately narrows it\nto only the \"creation failed\" case. Read that section before implementing; it is load\nbearing for `_try_create_github_issue`'s exact contract below.\n\n## Component contracts\n\n### `operations._try_create_github_issue` (currently operations.py:1444-1463)\n\n```python\ndef _try_create_github_issue(item_data: BacklogItem, repo: str, out: Output) -> tuple[int | None, str | None]:\n    \"\"\"Attempt to create a GitHub issue for item_data.\n\n    Args:\n        item_data: Populated BacklogItem (file_path may be empty at this stage).\n        repo: Repository slug (owner/name).\n        out: Output collector for warnings.\n\n    Returns:\n        ``(issue_number, None)`` on success.\n        ``(None, None)`` when GitHub is unavailable (no token, or ``try_get_github``\n        could not reach/resolve the repo) \u2014 this is the intentional local-only-create\n        fallback (#2999); it is not reported as an error because no GitHub attempt was\n        actually made to fail.\n        ``(None, reason)`` when a GitHub client was obtained but issue creation itself\n        raised ``GithubException``/``BacklogError`` \u2014 this is a genuine, actionable\n        failure and ``reason`` is the caller-visible message (#3182).\n    \"\"\"\n    repository = try_get_github(repo)\n    if repository is None:\n        out.warn(\"  WARNING: GitHub unavailable \u2014 creating local-only item\")\n        return None, None\n    try:\n        return create_issue_for_item(repository, item_data, dry_run=False, output=out), None\n    except (GithubException, BacklogError) as e:\n        reason = f\"Issue creation failed: {e}\"\n        out.warn(f\"  WARNING: {reason}\")\n        return None, reason\n```\n\nDiff from today: return type widens from `int | None` to `tuple[int | None, str | None]`;\neach `return` gains a second element; message text and control flow are otherwise\nunchanged.\n\n### `operations._try_create_backend_issue_ref` (currently operations.py:1466-1499)\n\n```python\ndef _try_create_backend_issue_ref(item_data: BacklogItem, repo: str, out: Output) -> tuple[str, str | None]:\n    \"\"\"Create a backend issue and return (issue ref, error reason or None).\n\n    Dispatches to the appropriate backend-native creation path:\n\n    - String-ID backends (beads): calls ``create_beads_issue_for_item`` on the\n      backend, returns the nanoid (e.g. ``\"bd-a3f8\"``). Failure here is unchanged \u2014\n      ``add_item``'s subsequent ``put_work_item`` call raises ``ContentUnavailableError``\n      for a still-issueless beads item (see ``BeadsBackend.put_work_item``); this\n      function's own return only ever carries ``None`` for beads' error slot.\n    - Integer-ID backends (GitHub, sqlite, memory): calls\n      ``_try_create_github_issue``, formats the returned number as ``\"#N\"``.\n\n    Returns:\n        ``(ref, None)`` on success, or on the tolerated GitHub-unavailable fallback.\n        ``(\"\", reason)`` only when the integer-ID path's own creation attempt raised \u2014\n        propagated verbatim from ``_try_create_github_issue``.\n\n    Args:\n        item_data: Populated BacklogItem (file_path may be empty at this stage).\n        repo: Repository slug (owner/name) \u2014 used by integer-ID backends.\n        out: Output collector for warnings.\n    \"\"\"\n    backend = get_config().backend\n    if backend.issue_id_type == \"string\":\n        from backlog_core.backends.beads_backend import BeadsBackend  # ruff: ignore[import-outside-top-level]\n\n        if isinstance(backend, BeadsBackend):\n            nanoid = backend.create_beads_issue_for_item(item_data, output=out)\n            return nanoid or \"\", None\n        out.warn(\"  WARNING: String-ID backend does not support create_beads_issue_for_item \u2014 creating local-only item\")\n        return \"\", None\n    issue_num, error = _try_create_github_issue(item_data, repo, out)\n    return (f\"#{issue_num}\", None) if issue_num else (\"\", error)\n```\n\nDiff from today: return type widens from `str` to `tuple[str, str | None]`; the\ninteger-ID branch's final line captures and forwards `error`; the two beads-side\nbranches are unchanged except for the added `, None`.\n\n### `operations.add_item` (currently operations.py:1528-1637)\n\nSignature and most of the body are unchanged. Only the call site and result\nconstruction change:\n\n```python\n    issue_ref, issue_error = _try_create_backend_issue_ref(item_data, repo, out)\n    item_reference = issue_ref or _resolve_reference(priority, slug)\n    ...\n    result: dict[str, str | int | bool | list[str]] = {\n        \"title\": title,\n        \"priority\": priority,\n        \"reference\": item_reference,\n        \"file_path\": item_reference,\n        \"item_ref\": issue_ref,\n    }\n    if issue_error:\n        result[\"error\"] = issue_error\n    return {**result, **out.to_dict()}\n```\n\nNo widening of the return type annotation is needed \u2014 `str` is already a member of\n`dict[str, str | int | bool | list[str]]`, and the key is omitted entirely (not set to\n`None`) when there is no error, matching `BacklogAddResponse.error: str | None = None`'s\ndefault-omitted-on-success convention already used by every other `FallibleToolResponse`\nsubclass in this file.\n\n**Docstring addition** (append to the existing `Returns:` block):\n\n> `error` is present only when an integer-ID backend obtained a GitHub client but issue\n> creation itself failed (see `_try_create_github_issue`); it is absent both on success\n> and on the tolerated GitHub-unavailable local-only-create path. Its presence never\n> changes whether the item was stored \u2014 the item is always persisted by the time this\n> function returns, `error` only adds a caller-visible reason for why `item_ref` is\n> empty in the \"attempted and failed\" sub-case.\n\n**Docstring addition** (`Raises:` block \u2014 one line, for completeness, no behavior\nchange): none needed; this path still never raises for the integer-ID case, which is\nthe point being documented above.\n\n### `BacklogAddResponse` (tool_responses.py:212-228)\n\nNo change. `error: str | None = None` is inherited from `FallibleToolResponse`\n(tool_responses.py:113-117) already.\n\n### MCP tool `backlog_add` (server.py:1377-1406)\n\nNo code change. `_respond(BacklogAddResponse, {**result, **out.to_dict()})`\n(server.py:1403) already calls `BacklogAddResponse.model_validate(payload).model_dump(exclude_none=True)`\n(`_respond`, server.py:147-190) \u2014 an `\"error\"` key present in `result` populates the\nmodel's existing `error` field exactly like the `except BacklogError` arm two lines\nbelow it does. Both arms produce the field the same way; only the source dict differs.\n\n**Docstring change** (the existing `Returns:` line \"On error, ``error`` is set.\" needs\none clause added):\n\n> On error, `error` is set \u2014 either because the call raised (duplicate item, invalid\n> priority/type) or, on an integer-ID backend, because GitHub issue creation itself\n> failed after a local-only item was already stored (`item_ref` will be `\"\"`, `reference`\n> still identifies the stored item).\n\n### CLI `backlog add` (sam_schema/backlog.py:38-76)\n\nOne code change: check the result dict's `error` key after the existing\n`except BacklogError` arm and exit non-zero, without changing what gets printed.\n\n```python\n@app.command(\"add\")\ndef add(\n    ...\n) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Add a new item to the backlog.\"\"\"\n    output = Output()\n    try:\n        result = operations.add_item(\n            title=title,\n            description=description,\n            priority=priority,\n            source=source,\n            type_=type_,\n            force=force,\n            repo=repo,\n            output=output,\n        )\n    except BacklogError as exc:\n        cli_output.exit_with_json_error({\"error\": str(exc)})\n    _emit(result, output)\n    if result.get(\"error\"):\n        raise typer.Exit(code=1)\n```\n\nRationale for keeping `_emit(result, output)` before the exit rather than routing\nthrough `cli_output.exit_with_json_error`: the item *was* created (unlike the\n`except BacklogError` arm, where nothing was created) \u2014 the caller still needs `title`,\n`reference`, and `file_path` from the JSON payload to find the local-only item later,\nnot just the error string. `typer.Exit(code=1)` matches `cli_output.exit_with_json_error`'s\nown default `exit_code=1` (`sam_schema/cli_output.py:74`), so both failure paths of this\ncommand now agree on exit code.\n\n**`_emit` itself is unchanged** \u2014 it already prints `output.errors` to stderr\n(sam_schema/backlog.py:22-23); this fix is purely about the exit code, since `_emit`\nnever controlled it.\n\n## Concerns (quality vigilance)\n\n1. **The Acceptance Criteria section, as groomed, is broader than this spec implements,\n   and implementing it literally would break a real, currently-passing test suite.**\n   `tests/test_frontend_parity_ops.py`'s `dh_env` fixture (line 99) explicitly sets\n   `GITHUB_TOKEN=\"\"` and `GH_TOKEN=\"\"` and drives `backlog add` through the CLI against a\n   `SQLiteBackend` \u2014 an integer-ID backend \u2014 in `test_backlog_add_parity`,\n   `test_query_filter_parity`, `test_query_filter_absent_key_returns_empty`,\n   `test_backlog_view_parity`, and others, all via `_invoke_cli`, which raises\n   `RuntimeError` on any non-zero CLI exit code. Every one of these tests exercises the\n   exact \"GitHub unavailable\" branch today and asserts (implicitly, by not failing) CLI\n   exit code 0. If `error` were populated (and the CLI exits non-zero) for\n   *\"GitHub unavailable\"* as well as *\"creation failed\"* \u2014 the literal reading of the\n   Acceptance Criteria's first bullet \u2014 this entire parity suite breaks. This spec\n   narrows the contract so only the \"obtained a GitHub client but creation itself\n   raised\" sub-case populates `error`; \"no client obtained at all\" stays a tolerated,\n   `error`-free fallback, exactly as `test_add_item_local_only_pending_state_visible_via_list_and_view`\n   already requires. **Recommend the Acceptance Criteria section be corrected during\n   implementation review** to read \"GitHub issue creation is attempted and fails\"\n   rather than \"fails or GitHub is unavailable\" \u2014 the current wording describes a\n   contract this codebase's own tests already forbid.\n2. **Likely pre-existing, unrelated bug, not in scope**: `ContentUnavailableError` (raised\n   by `BeadsBackend.put_work_item` on beads creation failure) is not a `BacklogError`\n   subclass and is not caught by either `backlog_add`'s or CLI `add`'s exception\n   handlers (both only catch `BacklogError`). A beads-backend `add_item` failure today\n   likely propagates as an unhandled exception through both production call sites\n   rather than the structured error response their docstrings promise. Verified by\n   reading `backlog_core/models.py:486-511` (class hierarchy) and grepping\n   `server.py`/`sam_schema/backlog.py` for `ContentUnavailableError` (absent from both).\n   Recommend a follow-up backlog item; fixing it here would exceed this item's scope\n   (integer-ID path only, per its own Impact Radius / RT-ICA condition 5).\n3. **`test_live_validation.py:196,318`** are named in the Acceptance Criteria as tests\n   that should \"pass, or fail with a message that shows the real reason\" \u2014 this spec's\n   contract makes `result.get(\"error\")` the real reason once it's populated, but the\n   test assertions themselves (not touched by this spec's file list) still need their\n   messages updated to include it, e.g.\n   `assert item_ref.startswith(\"#\"), f\"...got: {item_ref!r}, error: {result.get('error')!r}\"`.\n   Flagging so this isn't dropped \u2014 it's the one test-file edit implied but not\n   explicitly assigned to a component above.\n\n## Test contract\n\n### `test_add_item_item_ref_empty_when_no_github_issue` (test_backlog_core_operations.py:419-433)\n\nBecomes (mocks `try_get_github` returning `None` \u2014 the tolerated fallback, unchanged\nscenario, now also asserting `error` is *absent*, since this spec's contract keeps that\nsub-case silent):\n\n```python\ndef test_add_item_item_ref_empty_when_no_github_issue(self, mocker: MockerFixture) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Verify item_ref is empty and no error is set when GitHub is unavailable.\n\n    Tests: add_item item_ref contract on the local-only-create path (#2999), now\n           also covering that this tolerated fallback does not populate `error` (#3182).\n    How: Mock try_get_github to return None (no GitHub available).\n    Why: item_ref must always be present \u2014 its emptiness, not its absence, is\n         the local-only signal. GitHub-unavailable is a supported, silent fallback,\n         not a failure \u2014 `error` distinguishes it from the sibling case where a\n         GitHub client was obtained but creation itself raised.\n    \"\"\"\n    mocker.patch(\"backlog_core.operations.try_get_github\", return_value=None)\n\n    result = add_item(title=\"Local Only No Ref\", description=\"desc\", priority=\"P2\")\n\n    assert result[\"item_ref\"] == \"\"\n    assert \"error\" not in result\n```\n\n### New test: `test_add_item_error_set_when_issue_creation_raises` (add adjacent to the above)\n\n```python\ndef test_add_item_error_set_when_issue_creation_raises(self, mocker: MockerFixture) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Verify result[\"error\"] is populated when GitHub issue creation raises (#3182).\n\n    Tests: add_item's new caller-visible-failure contract \u2014 the defect this item fixes.\n    How: try_get_github succeeds (a client is obtained); create_issue_for_item raises\n         GithubException.\n    Why: This is the sub-case the CI defect (#3182) actually hits \u2014 a valid token but\n         a failed create call \u2014 and the one this fix's `error` field exists to surface.\n    \"\"\"\n    mocker.patch(\"backlog_core.operations.try_get_github\", return_value=mocker.Mock())\n    mocker.patch(\"backlog_core.operations.create_issue_for_item\", side_effect=GithubException(500, \"boom\", None))\n\n    result = add_item(title=\"Flaky Create\", description=\"desc\", priority=\"P1\")\n\n    assert result[\"item_ref\"] == \"\"\n    assert result.get(\"error\", \"\").startswith(\"Issue creation failed:\")\n```\n\n`GithubException` is already imported in this test file (`tests/test_backlog_core_operations.py:51`).\n\n### Unchanged (verify, do not edit)\n\n- `test_add_item_local_only_pending_state_visible_via_list_and_view` (435-459) \u2014 still\n  exercises the same GitHub-unavailable mock; still asserts no exception and a visible\n  pending state. This spec's contract keeps that path error-free, so this test needs no\n  change; it is the regression guard for Concern #1.\n- `TestAddItemBeadsBackend` (560-645) \u2014 beads dispatch untouched.\n- Every green-path test in `TestAddItem*` that mocks `create_issue_for_item` to\n  `return_value=<int>` \u2014 untouched; `_try_create_github_issue`'s success branch still\n  returns the same issue number, just paired with `None` instead of being a bare `int`.\n\n## Documentation contract\n\n### `skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/start.md`\n\n**Step 5** (currently lines 156-158) gains a failure branch:\n\n```markdown\n## Step 5: Use `item_ref` from the response\n\nThe `backlog_add` response contains `item_ref` \u2014 use that value directly for all downstream workflows and selector parameters.\n\nIf the response also carries a non-empty `error` (MCP field, or the CLI JSON payload's `\"error\"` key), the backend\nissue could not be created even though the item itself was stored: `item_ref` will be `\"\"` and `reference` is the\nonly selector for this item until an issue is created for it. Report the error text to the user, use `reference`\n(not `item_ref`) in Step 6's confirmation and next-step commands, and do not treat this as the generic\n\"MCP tool result contains `error`\" stop condition in Error handling below \u2014 the item exists and Step 6 still\napplies, just without an issue reference.\n```\n\n**Completion criteria** (currently lines 187-192), first bullet needs a parenthetical\nso it doesn't contradict the new Step 5 branch:\n\n```markdown\nCreation is complete only when:\n- `backlog_add` returns with no non-empty `errors` (a populated `error` field with GitHub-issue-creation-failure\n  text is not itself a stop condition \u2014 see Step 5's failure branch \u2014 but any other non-empty `error`/`errors` is)\n- `item_ref` is normalized to `#N`, or, if Step 5's failure branch applied, `reference` is used in its place\n- confirmation is shown to the user, using whichever selector (`item_ref` or `reference`) Step 5 resolved\n```\n\nNo other file in the Impact Radius's Documentation/Consumers sections needs changes \u2014\nconfirmed no other skill/agent/hook reads `add_item`/`backlog_add`'s `item_ref` or\n`error` fields (Impact Radius, \"Code \u2014 Consumers\").\n\n---\n\n## Post-Implementation Annotations\n\nAdded by context-refinement agent on 2026-09-04 (P8c3a5756/T6, quality-gate context refinement)\n\n### Design Refinements\n\n1. **Documentation contract refined beyond this spec's literal replacement text** \u2014 Step 5 / Error handling / Completion criteria in `create/start.md` needed a disambiguator this spec's verbatim text did not specify.\n   - Original (this spec, \"Documentation contract\" section above): Step 5's replacement text describes only the \"stored with error\" branch \u2014 `error` populated, item stored, `item_ref` empty, `reference` is the selector. It is silent on what happens when `error` is populated but the item was **never** stored (the pre-existing `except BacklogError` raise path, e.g. duplicate/validation error).\n   - Actual: commit `51823112c` (`fix(work-backlog-item): distinguish stored-with-error from never-stored in create doc`), made during the TN-verification-gate's correction pass rather than by the original T5 task, added an explicit disambiguator to Step 5, the Error-handling bullet, and Completion criteria \u2014 checking for a non-empty `reference`/`file_path`/`title` alongside `error` to distinguish \"stored with error\" (Step 5's carve-out, does not stop the workflow) from \"never stored\" (the generic stop condition, unchanged). This closes a real ambiguity the literal spec text left open, without changing this spec's design decision (only the \"creation raised\" sub-case ever populates `error` on a stored item \u2014 that decision is unaffected).\n   - Recorded in: P8c3a5756/T6 (context-refinement); also independently observed and endorsed by the doc-drift-audit artifact (`doc-drift-audit-qg-backlog-add-silent-failure`) and reviewed/passed by the T1 code-review artifact (`code-review-T1-qg-backlog-add-silent-failure`).\n   - Classification: design-refinement (auto-record) \u2014 same goal (route the doc-consuming agent correctly across the full three-outcome-plus-never-stored space), narrower/more complete implementation than this spec's literal text, no scope or goal change. No `Intent Source` reference exists on this artifact or on the feature-context artifact (pre-policy), so intent-divergence classification does not apply \u2014 see the dh:context-refinement skill's Step 5.4.\n\n2. **Impact Radius \"one documented consumer\" claim was one consumer short.** This spec's Documentation contract section states \"no other skill/agent/hook reads `add_item`/`backlog_add`'s `item_ref` or `error` fields.\" A second consumer, `skills/interop/SKILL.md` (Step 3 mermaid flow, lines 87-95), branches on the mere *presence* of a non-empty `error` key and ABORTs \u2014 a contradiction structurally identical to the one this spec's Documentation contract fixed in `create/start.md`, but not itself named as in-scope. Found and verified real during T5, routed to backlog item #3400 (linked to plan Pdea64bb9) rather than fixed under this item. Not a defect in this spec's analysis method, but the underlying Impact Radius search (grep for field names) missed a consumer that branches on key-presence rather than quoting the field name near `item_ref`/`error`. Recorded in: P8c3a5756/T6; item #3182 Concerns section (entries 2026-09-04T03:43:51Z, 04:27:44Z).\n"}